{
  "result": "At the edge of the city, where the last apartment buildings gave way to marsh grass, there was a bench painted an unconvincing blue.\n\nEvery morning, an old woman named Mara sat there with a thermos and a notebook. She did not write memoirs, although she had enough material for several. Instead, she recorded small events: a heron lifting from the reeds, the first bus groaning awake, a child in yellow boots discovering ice.\n\nHer neighbors assumed the notebook contained wisdom. They asked her what she was observing, hoping for advice about marriage, money, or grief. Mara would smile and say, \u201cToday the wind has changed its mind three times.\u201d\n\nSome found that answer annoying. Others returned the next day. Gradually, people began bringing their own notebooks. The accountant wrote down cloud shapes before work. The teenager described pigeons with unreasonable seriousness. A man whose wife had recently died listed the sounds of her garden: gate, robin, hose, bees.\n\nBy spring, the bench was surrounded by a quiet company of watchers. Nobody spoke much. They had learned that attention was not the same as solving. It was a way of standing beside the world without demanding that it explain itself.\n\nOne morning, Mara\u2019s bench was empty. Her thermos was gone, but her notebook rested beneath a stone. On the final page, she had written: \u201cPlease continue.\u201d\n\nThe others did. They watched the marsh darken, then brighten. They noticed new tracks in mud, a feather turning, and the arrival of rain.",
